et must run it hot, and a thermodynamic table sets a temperature limit. What happens to the sign of ΔG° as the reactor heats up, and why?", "model_answer": "ΔH° = −91.8 kJ, ΔS° = −198.1 J/K: ΔG° < 0 only below T = ΔH° ÷ ΔS° = 463 K. Above it, the −TΔS° term (positive) outweighs ΔH°: ΔG° = +46.9 kJ at 700 K. Industry runs hot for speed, at high pressure to compensate.", "target": "When ΔH° and ΔS° are both negative, the reaction is spontaneous only below T = ΔH° ÷ ΔS°.", "keywords": ["temperature", "−TΔS", "crossover", "low temperature", "entropy decreases", "exothermic", "outweighs", "sign changes"], "liquids": ["AR1", "AR2", "AR3", "AR4", "AR5", "AR6"], "kinds": ["crossover"], "fits": ["AR1", "AR2", "AR3", "AR4", "AR5", "AR6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch16/Ammonia_reactor.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "", "simulation": "ch16/free_energy_simulator.html?reaction={reaction}&T={T}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["Gibbs free energy", "temperature dependence", "crossover temperature"], "role": "exam", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, {"title": "The Fizzing Peroxide", "question": "Hydrogen peroxide in a brown bottle slowly breaks down into water and oxygen at any temperature, and an oxygen sensor in the cap finds O₂ building up month by month. Ozone, by contrast, never forms from oxygen on its own. Which sign combinations of ΔH° and ΔS° make a reaction spontaneous at every temperature, or at none?", "model_answer": "2H₂O₂(l) → 2H₂O(l) + O₂: ΔH° = −196.1 kJ, ΔS° = +126.0 J/K: ΔG° < 0 at all T (always). 3O₂ → 2O₃: ΔH° = +285.4 kJ, ΔS° = −137.8 J/K: ΔG° > 0 at all T (never). When ΔH and ΔS push ΔG the same way, temperature cannot flip the sign.", "target": "ΔH < 0 with ΔS > 0 is spontaneous at all temperatures; ΔH > 0 with ΔS < 0 at none.", "keywords": ["always", "never", "both favorable", "both unfavorable", "no crossover", "sign", "temperature cannot flip"